WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:07.859
What’s up guys?

00:00:07.859 --> 00:00:08.910
Jared again.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.639
Everyone’s been talking about it.

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:14.240
Every message board, every discord server,
every major news publication.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.949
We’ve even dipped our toes in on Culture
Binge.

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:21.410
You’ve probably heard of the Storm Area
51 Facebook Event, in which millions of net

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:26.550
nerds ironically vowed to Naruto run their
way into Area 51 on September 20th so they

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.670
can finally “see dem aliens.”

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:33.790
Now this is funny for a variety of reasons-
there’s nothing quite so satisfying as imagining

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:37.590
some general at the Pentagon being briefed
on what a group of Kyles is.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:43.620
But it’s also a dark joke about gleefully
signing up for, what is essentially a suicide

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:44.620
mission.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.850
Every 5 seconds a new meme pops up on the
Facebook event ironically strategizing their

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:53.780
entrance, taking inventory of vehicles and
infantry and, of course, coyly commenting

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:58.370
how people’s commitment to irony may outweigh
their desire to live.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:03.199
It’s this last class of memes that’s got
us thinking about a popular sub genre of memes

00:01:03.199 --> 00:01:06.619
dominating the land of shitposting: Depression
memes.

00:01:06.619 --> 00:01:10.490
Whether it’s people identifying with Bart
Simpson as literally nothing hits him with

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:15.490
a wave of incredible sadness or a young boy
being visited by Teletubbies labeled anxiety,

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:19.580
depression, loneliness, and overthinking,
depression memes seem to be millenials and

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.439
Gen Zers new form of free therapy.

00:01:22.439 --> 00:01:25.610
But the more we thought about it, the more
we thought that maybe there’s something

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:30.130
else to it, that maybe depression memes can
be considered the most modern development

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:35.490
of a certain philosophical and psychological
sentiment described over 150 years ago in

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.390
what is considered one of the first existentialist
stories.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:41.400
So welcome to this Wisecrack Vlog on Depression
Memes.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.369
And for the love of god don’t try to storm
area 51.

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:45.369
Please.

00:01:45.369 --> 00:01:48.160
But if you do, send me pics of dem aliens.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:42.560
So what is a shitpost?

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:46.790
There are a bunch of definitions out there
that often include the idea that they’re

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:51.800
deliberately unproductive to online conversation,
that they exist for their own sake, and/or

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:54.349
are made hastily and in poor quality.

00:02:54.349 --> 00:02:58.349
But the definition we found most insightful
comes from writer Gustavo Turner in Surface

00:02:58.349 --> 00:03:04.350
magazine: “What is a shitpost if not a pointless,
carnivalesque attack against meaning itself?

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:10.190
Like every aesthetic that draws from the absurd
(Dada, surrealism, situationism, punk), shitposting

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:14.540
is ultimately a cry of despair against impending
disaster.”

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:19.269
While Turner is apt in likening the shitpost
to various absurdist art movements, we instead

00:03:19.269 --> 00:03:22.909
find a very specific flavor of existentialism
in the depression meme.

00:03:22.909 --> 00:03:27.220
It’s our contention that this phenomenon
of people posting memes about their despair

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:32.540
is an extension of a philosophical and psychological
sentiment immortalized by Fyodor Dostoevsky’s

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:34.620
novella “Notes from Underground.”

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:39.540
Or more simply, the titular Underground Man
is functionally a 19th century proto-shitposter.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:43.350
To find out what we’re talking about, we’re
gonna have to dive in to a little recap of

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:44.350
the book.

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:48.770
It portrays the curmudgeonly musings of The
Underground Man, a tortured recluse who reminisces

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:52.090
about a time he almost had an authentic connection
with another human.

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:55.519
It’s starts off with him proclaiming that
“he is sick.”

00:03:55.519 --> 00:03:59.519
Not because he’s suffering from any physical
ailments, but rather from “hyperconsciousness,”

00:03:59.519 --> 00:04:03.660
or an extreme debilitating commitment to rationality.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:07.159
The first part of the book depicts his stream
of consciousness as he weaves an intricate

00:04:07.159 --> 00:04:12.720
labyrinth of arguments about topics like reason,
freedom, and European Utopianism before recounting

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.140
an unpleasant interaction with his old schoolmate
Zverkov.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:20.269
He eventually meets a prostitute named Liza,
who he tries to “redeem” by telling her

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:21.430
to change her ways.

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:26.360
She’s able to get him to open up for a little
while, but he quickly turns back into an asshole

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.090
and tries to humiliate her.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:33.270
As she leaves, he immediately regrets his
behavior and runs after her, but she’s long

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:34.270
gone.

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:37.000
The Underground Man has remained supremely
relevant over time.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.830
Dostoevsky’s metaphor of “the underground”
was a major development in modern literature

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:45.960
and continues to be referenced as an embodiment
of rebellion against traditional value systems.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:50.030
Which is all well and good, but what does
this extremely lonely and extremely miserable

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:54.190
man have to do with people on the internet
joking about the disposability of their lives

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:56.139
via Area 51 memes?

00:04:56.139 --> 00:05:00.060
We think that the state of despair propagated
by depression memes overlaps with the sentiment

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.910
of the Underground Man’s in four key ways:

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:03.910
1.

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:04.910
Inaction.

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:09.050
Both depression memes and the Underground
Man emphasize despair over action.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:12.550
The point of posting a meme like the cat in
the hat with the text “millennials and get

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:16.930
z’s willingness to die” sneaking up on
a kid standing in for “Area 51 thinking

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:21.530
this is a joke” isn’t to inspire the masses
to get off their computer and help Millennials

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:26.440
or Gen Zers by pushing for economic reform
or greater public mental health options.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:30.240
These memes are often born out of reflecting
on what seems like an impossible, hopeless

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.250
situation with no particular orientation towards
doing anything about it.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:38.180
The meme only offers a sense of identification
with hopelessness.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:40.160
The futility of action is key.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:46.070
Likewise, the much of the Underground Man’s
deep unhappiness is due to what he calls “Hyperconsciousness”-

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:48.139
or a paralyzing sense of awareness.

00:05:48.139 --> 00:05:53.250
He claims to be so aware that he sees both
sides of any argument, so instead of choosing

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:58.160
a side or defining himself through any meaningful
action, he does nothing.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:05:59.889
He calls this “conscious inertia.”

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:04.520
“It was not only that I could not become
spiteful, I did not know how to become anything:

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:10.450
neither spiteful nor kind, neither a rascal
nor an honest man, neither a hero nor an insect.

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:14.850
Now, I am living out my life in my corner,
taunting myself with the spiteful and useless

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:19.789
consolation that an intelligent man cannot
become anything seriously, and it is only

00:06:19.789 --> 00:06:21.789
the fool who becomes anything.”

00:06:21.789 --> 00:06:26.259
Like our melancholy hero, depression memes
are defined by a similar kind of inertia-

00:06:26.259 --> 00:06:28.900
a feeling of hopeless paralysis.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:29.919
2.

00:06:29.919 --> 00:06:33.270
Like a good shitpost, The Underground Man
is kind of a troll.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:37.410
When he visits his old friend Zverkov, he
KNOWS that he and his friends don’t like

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:41.789
him, but tags along to a party anyway and
acts like a drunken fool just to spread the

00:06:41.789 --> 00:06:43.039
misery around.

00:06:43.039 --> 00:06:48.380
Similarly, a depression shitpost looks not
only to express despair, but pervert the sunny

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:51.550
optimism of traditional social media posts.

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:55.949
If most people on Instagram are curating their
social media personas to project an image

00:06:55.949 --> 00:07:00.979
of a perfect life with attractive, successful,
and supportive friends, a depression meme

00:07:00.979 --> 00:07:04.410
looks to bring the grimy underbelly of feelings
to the forefront.

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:08.449
Think you’re accumulating cultural capital
by posting about your sweet beachfront vacation

00:07:08.449 --> 00:07:10.180
spot with your sexy rich family?

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:11.180
Fuck you.

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:12.979
I eat depression for breakfast.

00:07:12.979 --> 00:07:14.440
3.

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:17.830
Like The Underground Man, a Depression Meme
is a paradox.

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:21.910
Part of what has made the psychological profile
of the Underground Man so interesting and

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:25.470
enduring is that he is constantly contradicting
himself.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:29.639
He says he loves being isolated, yet he yearns
for human connection.

00:07:29.639 --> 00:07:34.720
Similarly, a depression meme is a cry for
help, yet a resignation to hopelessness.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:40.470
The Underground Man looks down on normal people
like Liza and Zverkov, yet envies them, not

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:45.500
unlike how a depression meme is a condemnation
of normie-dom, yet a desire to live a life

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.500
like theirs.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.319
Finally, the Underground Man suffers, but
takes pleasure in it.

00:07:50.319 --> 00:07:53.620
And of course- people make depression memes
for the lulz.

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:56.990
Why, suffering is the sole origin of consciousness.

00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:00.550
Though I did lay it down at the beginning
that consciousness is the greatest misfortune

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:05.680
for man, yet I know man prizes it and would
not give it up for any satisfaction.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:06.680
4.

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:12.220
Finally, both the Underground Man and shitposting
represent a rejection of optimistic political

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:13.220
ideals.

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:16.770
The Underground Man spends a fair amount of
time refuting ideas put forward by Nikolai

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:21.099
Chernyshvsky’s image of a utopia built out
of a devotion to reason that he referred to

00:08:21.099 --> 00:08:25.099
as “The Crystal Palace” in his work “What
is to be Done?”

00:08:25.099 --> 00:08:30.250
Similarly, depressing memes are often a reaction
to the post-Cold War 90s- a decade built on

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:34.930
the promise of a better world, but, to many,
that didn’t happen and now we only look

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:39.890
forward to “sunny” possibilities like
climate catastrophes, war with Iran, and Silicon

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:43.600
Valley ruining everything under the guise
of “disruption.”

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:47.370
Like the Underground Man, Millennials and
Gen Zers have grown up with the promise of

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:52.990
a better future, only to be hit with sobering
realities that that has made us… depressed.

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:57.500
As such, depression memes will often co-opt
positive elements of popular culture and impose

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:59.710
sad realities on them via text.

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:04.760
In doing so, it comments on the failure of
these images to deliver any actual happiness,

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:07.860
and the lie of positivity that they perpetuated.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:12.649
So there you have it- The Underground man
is very much alive in the ethos of depression

00:09:12.649 --> 00:09:14.170
memes on the internet.

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:18.720
But I want to emphasize here that depression
and other mental illnesses are a very serious

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:23.170
thing, and while some may find levity in these
kinds of things, it’s not cathartic for

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:24.170
everyone.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:27.920
For what it’s worth, I always remind myself
of the book’s end, where the Underground

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:30.690
Man deeply regrets rejecting human companionship.

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:35.279
Yes, the state of things might be dismal and
derailing meaning and trolling might give

00:09:35.279 --> 00:09:39.370
us the necessary dopamine hit to get through
the day, but it’s important that we don’t

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:43.580
give up an incredibly important part of a
healthy psyche: human connection.

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:44.580
 

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:47.720
And I just wanna end this vlog with a quote
from the text that will undeniably speak to

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:50.100
anyone who’s ever posted a depression meme:

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:55.510
“For we are all divorced from life, we are
all cripples, every one of us, more or less.

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:59.860
We are so divorced from it that we feel at
once a sort of loathing for real life, and

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:02.020
so cannot bear to be reminded of it.

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:07.089
Why, we have come almost to look upon real
life as an effort, almost as hard work, and

00:10:07.089 --> 00:10:10.330
we are all privately agreed that it is better
in books.”

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:13.800
Thanks to all of our patrons who support the
channel and our podcasts.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.759
